  body: "# Reduced Earnings Allowance and earning capacity\n\n## Authored interpretation\n\nREA is described as
    a benefit in its own right compensating loss of earning capacity following industrial accident or prescribed
    disease, rather than a comparison of all household income with former wages.\n\n## Scope and exceptions\n\n\
    The source has pre-1 October 1990 accident and later disease-prescription boundaries. Its benefit-plus-earnings
    example is not a means test for Pension Credit or UC. Assessment, relevant loss of faculty, regular occupation,
    equivalent employment and rate limits remain separate.\n\n## Inspected passages\n\n- [DMG 71001, PDF page 7](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5d11f8c6e5274a065e721738/dmgch71.pdf#page=7).\n\
    - [DMG 71002, PDF page 7](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5d11f8c6e5274a065e721738/dmgch71.pdf#page=7).\n\
    - [DMG 71003, PDF page 7](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5d11f8c6e5274a065e721738/dmgch71.pdf#page=7).\n\
    - [DMG 71011, PDF page 8](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5d11f8c6e5274a065e721738/dmgch71.pdf#page=8).\n\
    \n## Evidence and review status\n\nIndependent experimental interpretation of captured DWP guidance, not an
    official DWP document. Definitions and semantic associations are model-assisted proposals awaiting specialist
    review. No individual entitlement decision, medical conclusion, award calculation or executable rule is provided.\n\
    \nSource observation, listing-page update, printed revision, legal event and claimant-event dates are separate.
    Current legal applicability is unresolved. Page numbers are one-based PDF positions; machine extraction may
    damage reading order, characters and footnotes. No visual PDF or specialist acceptance is claimed.\n\n- `dmg-vol11-ch71`:
    PDF `e001c101fb7be9bc739a8a5b9c1281637a93f34a099658e7dafef6fbb2b5d4f5`; extracted pages `1414e514cf78c7a2e0a3ce66e2d88727d3fda28f764fd2e8654361dc569356d6`;
    acquired `2026-09-15T19:45:34.428591Z`.\n"
  resource: https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5d11f8c6e5274a065e721738/dmgch71.pdf#page=7
  semantic_relations:
  - target: term/rea-regular-occupation
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: The source measures loss of earning capacity against the claimant’s regular occupation and
      suitable equivalent employment.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol11-ch71/0007
      quote: "71002   REA compensates for the loss of earnings capacity where, as a result of an IA or\n       \
        \ PD, employed earners are unable to\n\n        1.    return to their regular occupation or\n\n        2.\
        \    carry out other work producing as high a level of earnings."
      locator: DMG 71002
    - page: page/dmg-vol11-ch71/0011
      quote: "71041   One of the conditions for the award of REA is that the claimant is incapable of\n        following
        their regular occupation. It is therefore necessary to establish whether the\n        claimant had a regular
        occupation and, if so, what it was. This must usually be\n        considered in relation to the date of
        the accident or date of onset of the disease1.\n        This date is important and is referred to as “the
        relevant date”.\n                                                                                      \
        \     1 R(I) 18/60"
      locator: DMG 71041
  - target: term/rea-entitlement-gap
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: The chapter distinguishes gaps in entitlement from non-payment and limits the historical gap
      rule.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol11-ch71/0008
      quote: "71012   A person who\n\n        1.    is entitled to REA on 30.9.90 and\n\n        2.    ceases to
        be entitled to REA for one or more days from 1.10.90\n\n        cannot again become entitled to REA for
        that accident1 or PD (but see DMG 71015).\n                                                            \
        \                    1 SS CB Act 92, Sch 7, para 11(2)"
      locator: DMG 71012
    - page: page/dmg-vol11-ch71/0008
      quote: "71013   REA can still be awarded where\n\n        1.    a continuation claim is made for a period
        beginning on the day following the\n              last day of entitlement and\n\n        2.    the DM accepts
        that the entitlement conditions have been continuously\n              satisfied since 1.10.901.\n\n    \
        \    There may still be underlying entitlement to REA even though REA is not payable.\n                \
        \                                                                1 SS CB Act 92, Sch 7, para 11(2)"
      locator: DMG 71013
    - page: page/dmg-vol11-ch71/0009
      quote: "71015   A person who was not entitled to REA on 30.9.90, for example, initial entitlement\n      \
        \  starts after that date, is not affected by DMG 71012 and gaps in entitlement from\n                 \
        \                                        1\n        1.10.90 have no effect on future REA entitlement .\n\
        \                                                                     1 SS CB Act 92, Sch 7, para 11(2)"
      locator: DMG 71015
